0.150.28Instead, since Geiger states "the ankle actuator system 17 actuates foot 19 relative tolower leg 18 at joint 20," and since the Examiner equated the lower leg 18 and the foot 19 of Geiger to the claimed first and second links, the ankle actuator system 17 should be equated to the claimed module. However, Geiger shows only one ankle actuator system 17 for one leg. Even if there are two ankle actuator systems 17 for the right and left legs in Geiger, Geiger still fails to teach or suggest that such systems 17 are coupled together.

SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.150.28Further, the electro hydrostatic actuators 30A, 30B of Geiger are actuators, not modules.Thus, each of the electro hydrostatic actuators 30A, 30B is not configured to include a first link, a second link, and a liquid pressure cylinder. As is clear from FIGs. 1 and 3 of Geiger above, the actuator 30A is connected to connection 26A of the leg portion 18 and to connection 28A of the foot portion 19 to rotate foot portion 19 left or right about roll axis 25 relative to lower leg portion 18 (para 0032 of Geiger). That is, the actuators 30A, 30B do not include the leg portion 18 and foot portion 19.

SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.150.28Furthermore, although the Examiner fails to determine which element is equated to theclaimed cylinder chamber and the cylinder block, Geiger states "[e]ach piston 37A, 37B sealingly separates left chamber 35A, 35B from right chamber 36A, 36B, respectively" (para 0028 of Geiger). Thus, chambers 35A, 35B, 36A, 36B may be equated to the claimed cylinder chamber. However, as shown in FIG. 11 of Geiger, the chambers 35A, 35B, 36A, 36B are formed in cylindrical housings 34A, 36B that are included in the actuators 30A, 30B. That is, the actuators 30A, 30B have the chambers 35A, 35B, 36A, 36B, but the leg portion 18 does not. Accordingly, Geiger does not teach or suggest "the first link includes a cylinder block in which the cylinder chamber of the liquid pressure cylinder is formed," as recited in claim 1.
